Docker volumes provide persistent storage that survives container restarts and deletions.


Create a named Docker volume
#docker volume create <volume_name>

List all Docker volumes
#docker volume ls

View detailed information about a volume
#docker volume inspect <volume_name>

Remove a Docker volume
#docker volume rm <volume_name>

Remove all unused Docker volumes
#docker volume prune

Mount a named volume into a container
#docker run -v <volume_name>:<container_path> <image_name>

Bind mount the current host directory into a container
#docker run -v $(pwd):<container_path> <image_name>

Mount volumes using explicit mount syntax
#docker run --mount type=bind,source=<host_path>,target=<container_path> <image_name>

Mount volumes from another container
#docker run --volumes-from <container_name> <image_name>

